      "updated_at": "2018-05-23T05:37:56Z",
      "body": "Maybe I can answer some of these questions.\r\n\r\n> used an email, then close his mailbox and then someone else might have open the same email address (I'm not sure that's authorized by email providers though so not sure about that argument)\r\n\r\nThis is possible. Some email providers make the address available straight after deletion, most block it for a few months and some providers (Gmail) block it forever.\r\n\r\n> the real user would have received an order confirmation email and would have write to us saying \"I never ordered, how did you get my email, etc.\"\r\n\r\nIf I received an email like that, I would suspect some kind of scam. Maybe I would mark it as spam, but I wouldn't react to it, because that is how the bad guys get you.\r\n\r\nI agree that the cases in which we don't want to associate the data are very rare. But they can happen and we would be doing the wrong thing in these cases. These issues have been real for bigger companies with more customers and therefore more email addresses. For me, this comes down to basic security measures. When you order as a guest, there is no authentication and all data is untrusted. We can display that to the manager for their information, but it's not right to display it to customers.",
      "author_association": "MEMBER",
      "pin": null,
